I don't share that view.

There are balance issue, pudding farming and effectiveness of late E-
may be prominent examples. And I understand if one takes the categorical
view that a game is non-balanced as long as it supports such features.
My view is that I can avoid pudding farming by abstaining to exploit
it, because I think it has been an oversight. (Okay, I don't abstain
from using the E word, but so what? The game is still challenging for
me. You and David may be better and more experienced players. <shrug>)
If I talk about balance I don't slam the whole thing because of one of
the few prominent examples where something has been overseen, rather I
see the balancing design issues in really a lot of details during my
games, where I often think; "well done". While you seem to take them
for granted, I'm hardly able to recall every detail, so many there are.
Off top of my head some that have been mentioned here recently; class
and race properties (e.g. WRT two-weaponing), exclusive gear and/or AK
selection (limited slots, you cannot have "all", objects well tuned in
that respect), object behaviour (you cannot just use anything new, you
have to take some precautions), non-E-respecting creatures, etc.

